include README.md
include LICENSE-APACHE
include LICENSE-MIT
include NOTICE
# Root artifacts the shipped suite reads, for the same reason `docs` is grafted below:
# GOVERNANCE.md is what tools/check_public_boundary.py asks every tree who maintains it,
# CONFORMANCE_CONSUMER.json is the pin tests/test_conformance_consumer_prose.py holds the
# README to, and CONTRIBUTING.md is where tests/test_readme_quickstart.py finds the link to
# docs/maintainers/, CHANGELOG.md is where tools/release_checks.py finds the section of the
# version, and CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md and SECURITY.md are the two documents
# tests/test_reporting_channels.py holds to the same reporting channels. Without them the shipped tests that read them fail on extraction. The
# rule is one rule: the source distribution ships every root artifact its own tests and tools
# read, and tests/test_release_checks_0_1_0.py fails when one of them is added without this
# file following.
include CHANGELOG.md
include CONFORMANCE_CONSUMER.json
include CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
include CONTRIBUTING.md
include GOVERNANCE.md
include SECURITY.md
# The sdist carries the proofs; the wheel does not change.
#
# MANIFEST.in governs the source distribution only: the wheel is the same either way, byte
# for byte the artifact `local_wheel_gate.py --check-reproducible` prints a
# digest for, so nobody who installs the wheel pays for this. The proofs reach only someone
# who deliberately asked for the source, which is exactly a reviewer doing diligence, and
# this package's whole thesis is that a claim should be checkable by the recipient rather than
# taken on trust. This comment gives no size or member count for either artifact: the
# wheel's would go stale at the next change to a shipped source file, and the source
# distribution's cannot be recorded in a file the source distribution ships without the
# record moving what it records. The gate prints the wheel's digest on every run.
#
# Measured, not assumed, in the one configuration the gate runs: `local_wheel_gate.py
# --check-dist` extracts the sdist into a fresh virtual environment holding the sdist and
# pytest, with no sibling repository beside it and LOQUITUR_REQUIRE_SIBLINGS removed from the
# child environment rather than left to whoever runs the gate. There it
# passes with none failed. Most skips are the narrative tests and the
# cryptography-backed references, which need the optional loquitur-schemas extra, and the rest
# need a sibling checkout. The counts move as tests are added and the gate prints the current
# ones on every run, so none is recorded here: nothing checks a figure in a comment.
# `docs` is grafted because tests read docs/maintainers/build-checks.md; without it the
# shipped suite fails on extraction, which would be worse than shipping no tests at all.
graft tests
graft tools
graft docs
# The one third-party file of the repository stays out of the source distribution. NOTICE: it is
# under the Apache License, Version 2.0 only, so a distribution carrying it would not be
# `MIT OR Apache-2.0` as its License-Expression declares (PEP 639: the expression covers every
# file the distribution holds). The wheel never carried it. In an extracted sdist the tests that
# read it skip and say why; in the repository its absence fails them.
exclude tests/vectors/ecdsa_secp256k1_sha256_p1363_test.json
# global-exclude, not recursive-exclude. `recursive-exclude __pycache__ *` matches a
# directory of that name at the top level and nothing nested, so grafting tests would ship
# .pyc files compiled by one machine into an artifact a package index keeps forever.
# global-exclude applies at any depth.
global-exclude *.py[cod]
global-exclude __pycache__/*
prune .pytest_cache
prune .ruff_cache
prune .mypy_cache
prune build
prune dist
prune htmlcov
